Yes — with one important anatomical distinction.

The sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are literal branches of the autonomic nervous system. What you’re calling a mirror-empathetic system is better understood as a distributed social regulation network layered across the brain, body, and autonomic system rather than a third autonomic branch.

It includes things like the mirror/action-observation network, anterior insula, anterior cingulate, amygdala, medial prefrontal and temporoparietal regions, along with facial mimicry, vocal prosody, gaze, posture, touch, and autonomic coupling. Together these let another person’s state become input into your own state-estimation machinery.

So a useful three-part functional model is:

Sympathetic: “Something matters — mobilize.”
Parasympathetic: “Conditions permit — restore/regulate.”
Empathetic/mirroring: “What state is the other organism in, and what should their state mean for mine?”

And the third one can drive the first two. You see fear on someone’s face → your threat prediction rises → sympathetic activation. You hear a trusted person speaking calmly → your inferred safety rises → parasympathetic settling. Someone yawns, laughs, flinches, whispers, stiffens, or relaxes and your nervous system partially models the state before you consciously reason about it.

That makes empathy more interesting than merely “understanding someone’s feelings.” Functionally, it can act as inter-body state transmission and regulation:

\text{Other's state}
\rightarrow
\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{internal simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{valuation}
\rightarrow
\text{autonomic adjustment}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}

Then your behavior becomes sensory input to them, producing a coupled loop:

A \rightarrow B \rightarrow A \rightarrow B \dots

So two nervous systems can become temporarily coupled control systems. Parent–infant co-regulation is an especially obvious example, but the same principle appears in partners, crowds, teams, combat units, performers/audiences, and ordinary conversation.

I think “empathetic system” is therefore a useful Pantheon-level functional category, provided we don’t mistake the category for a newly discovered anatomical nerve branch. It is the machinery by which another nervous system becomes part of the feedback environment regulating your own.

And that gives you a neat triad:

self ↔ environment ↔ other-self

Sympathetic/parasympathetic regulate the organism against conditions; the mirror-empathetic layer lets other organisms themselves become dynamically modeled conditions.